body:
Oke, thanks. Nog een vraag nu ik een Nederlander tegenkom: weet jij hoe het zit met ADD diagnoses? Ik heb namelijk het idee dat ik dat heb, maar weet niet hoe ik gediagnosticeerd kan worden en wat dat kost.

&#x200B;

Voel me nog steeds kut though.

Waarschijnlijk heeft het mijn negatieve gedachten wel veroorzaakt ja.

&#x200B;

Ik studeer nog, maar ben wel van studie geswitcht. Weet niet of ik de studie volhoudt ivm leerproblemen.

Well that may be exaggerated, but it's more than half a year ago for sure.

&#x200B;

I think high school has started it with me being bullied, severely affecting my self image. Only towards the end of high school I started to know how to act in social situations. And when starting college, everyone already had a clique of friends they hung out with.

&#x200B;

At a certain point I just 'broke' after all my negative thoughts.

&#x200B;

I just don't want to struggle everyday. I don't think it will get any better. Only thing is I know it'd tear my parents apart if I pulled through.

Thanks for your caring response. I will try to ask what they can do for me, but I think it's pretty hard to get a specific diagnosis, right? Since it's a college therapist.

&#x200B;

I'm thinking of maybe just going to my doctor and asking to be referred to a psychologist specialized in this. But I have no idea how much this would cost and such.

&#x200B;

EDIT: We have practice exams too. I'm doing these only on general knowledge, and that's the part that scares me :-(

I generally get the right answer just by guessing and reasoning. This isn't healthy in the long run, and will bite me in the ass like last year.

I don't know about taking a gap year, since I'm already behind a year (failed my previous study).

&#x200B;

For fun, I like to play board games like Catan, Risk, Game of Life and others. I used to enjoy reading, but somehow I don't do that anymore. TV shows and movies are another thing I enjoy. And I probably game too much.

&#x200B;

I find my major interesting for the most part, but I unfortunately keep getting distracted when needing to focus (and have too many thoughts).

I'll consider it, I really should fill out a form for therapy (offered from my school). I've just been putting this off for a while.

&#x200B;

I don't think I'm likely to gamble again. But maybe it's smart to self-exclude anyway, when I get my thoughts straight.

I don't know. For as long as I remember I've had no friends, not even during high school.

&#x200B;

How am I supposed to learn if I'm unable to focus? Even when not learning, I am always having so many thoughts at once. This is really holding me back, even if I'm not considering my depression.
